  journal  = "Genetics",
  volume   =  211,
  number   =  4,
  pages    = "1125--1130",
  abstract = "The genetics and evolution of complex traits, including
              quantitative traits and disease, have been hotly debated ever
              since Darwin. A century ago, a paper from R.A. Fisher reconciled
              Mendelian and biometrical genetics in a landmark contribution that
              is now accepted as the main foundation stone of the field of
              quantitative genetics. Here, we give our perspective on Fisher's
              1918 paper in the context of how and why it is relevant in today's
              genome era. We mostly focus on human trait variation, in part
